18. SCORING & ANALYTICS ENGINE

Implement actual calculation modules rather than static values.

All calculations must be clearly documented.

The numbers should be internally consistent and update predictably when the data changes.

Recovery Score

Use a custom, transparent heuristic score.

Possible component weighting for a complete demo dataset:

- 30% HRV relative to personal baseline
- 20% Resting heart rate relative to baseline
- 25% Sleep-related recovery contribution
- 15% Recent training load / fatigue
- 10% Subjective recovery factors

These are illustrative product-design weights, not scientifically validated clinical coefficients.

Normalize individual components carefully.

Handle missing values by explicitly reducing confidence and reweighting valid components within reasonable bounds.

Do not silently insert normal values when observations are missing.

Important:

A high HRV value is not automatically better in every situation.

Use contextual individual trends and avoid simplistic universal thresholds.

Sleep Score

Create an explainable heuristic combining:

- Sleep duration relative to target
- Regularity
- Efficiency
- Sleep continuity

Stage information may be used only if available and reliable.

Use conditional weighting for missing information.

Training Load

Create a coherent load model that considers:

- Session duration
- Relative intensity
- Heart rate distribution
- Sport
- Perceived effort
- Available performance information

Store raw or intermediate training load separately from any normalized 0–100 presentation score.

Avoid simply adding normalized daily scores together.

Personal baselines

Implement:

- Rolling baseline estimation
- Minimum required observations
- Outlier handling
- Trend calculations
- Data quality checks
- Confidence estimates

